                    {
                        "translation_id": "171aa34f-b581-4001-86d7-007f1b2b416a",
                        "source_entry_id": "81a5f18e-4e9c-34c7-b877-036a468c0c24",
                        "source_sense_id": "31fedb81-ff3b-3930-bba2-d4b9876095b1",
                        "target_lang_word": "structuralism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "structuralism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e4761797-9428-3d94-92d7-ff19aba99ee8",
                                        "entry_id": "67f4e45c-96b7-3467-a2ac-80e787827684",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A theory of sociology that views elements of society as part of a cohesive, self-supporting structure."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "58a6ce44-8e61-3108-b8c7-1e80ddae3fa0",
                                        "entry_id": "67f4e45c-96b7-3467-a2ac-80e787827684",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A school of biological thought that deals with the law-like behaviour of the structure of organisms and how it can change, emphasising that organisms are wholes, and therefore that change in one part must necessarily take into account the inter-connected nature of the entire organism."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "266e6cd3-2c1e-3423-8bae-c8b2bfd4d449",
                                        "entry_id": "67f4e45c-96b7-3467-a2ac-80e787827684",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The theory that a human language is a self-contained structure related to other elements which make up its existence."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d0341689-840b-3237-ba7f-bcc261df319c",
                                        "entry_id": "67f4e45c-96b7-3467-a2ac-80e787827684",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A school of thought that focuses on exploring the individual elements of consciousness, how they are organized into more complex experiences, and how these mental phenomena correlate with physical events."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "73ee5d26-f426-368f-aee8-c6d51c4a0b66",
                                        "entry_id": "67f4e45c-96b7-3467-a2ac-80e787827684",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "In the philosophy of mathematics, a theory that holds that mathematical theories describe structures, and that mathematical objects are exhaustively defined by their place in such structures."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "196d9cb0-0720-4276-87d3-4dce1135784b",
                        "source_entry_id": "81a5f18e-4e9c-34c7-b877-036a468c0c24",
                        "source_sense_id": "31fedb81-ff3b-3930-bba2-d4b9876095b1",
                        "target_lang_word": "structuralisme",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "structuralisme",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "559136da-c6d4-3b20-a853-ba3104a57bd2",
                                        "entry_id": "0ac847e4-1b83-3e12-9cd4-c96bd5ac1daf",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Courant des sciences humaines qui s’inspire du modèle linguistique et appréhende la réalité sociale comme un ensemble formel de relations."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "12db7dd4-a8cd-418b-a2c3-bc1d3ed198e6",
                                                "sense_id": "559136da-c6d4-3b20-a853-ba3104a57bd2",
                                                "text": "Le structuralisme a permis la position d’un inconscient structural linguistiquement articulé, dans le psychisme, dans les mythes, dans les représentations collectives ou les textes littéraires. Il a posé une pensée d’avant la pensée, condition anonyme de nos créations, qui de ce fait ne peuvent se comprendre que comme des variations sur un thème imposé, ou des combinaisons formées à partir d’un inventaire que nous n’avons pas choisi."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3b3b584e-5a34-3401-8fbe-61963f5daea9",
                                        "entry_id": "0ac847e4-1b83-3e12-9cd4-c96bd5ac1daf",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Approche critique, surtout en vogue dans les années 1970 et 1980, qui consiste à faire complètement abstraction de l'auteur et du contexte de rédaction pour interpréter un texte."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "especially"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8372b0da-2d1f-47e7-bb4f-b4175578613f",
                                                "sense_id": "3b3b584e-5a34-3401-8fbe-61963f5daea9",
                                                "text": "C'était l'époque du structuralisme caractérisé par l'étude du texte par le texte pour le texte, qui refusait tout recours à la subjectivité pour en expliquer le sens. La biographie de l'auteur n'était pas abordée, ni le contexte d'énonciation de l'œuvre, ni ses conditions de production."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
